Z||000005||Phil||01-13-2000||02:19 PM||||Terryk<BR>I believe that those hp readings were taken at the wheels. The stock 2.8 gave 116hp and the SC gave 201hp that's almost double. Rule of thumb says about 20% loss through the drivetrain. If you play the numbers game that yields 145hp stock and 251 SC at the crank. I would like to see the torque # because that's what really counts. They must really be putting the boost to it though to gain that much, don't know how long it would last.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||01-13-2000||02:52 PM||||I don't get it. I was under the impression a supercharger was much more efficient than a turbocharger and would give you a 100hp boost or so...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||01-13-2000||04:29 PM||||Technically turbochargers are more efficient than superchargers by the mere fact that they are not parasitic, meaning they do not use crank hp to run.  You can however get more boost from a roots type supercharger than you can get from a centrifugal turbocharger.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||01-13-2000||04:36 PM||||Phil,<P>I believe they have their supercharger set at 9 lbs. of boost.  Not terribly high, but high enough, I guess.<P>They (RSM) use a Rotrex supercharger....does anyone have any data on them or any opinions about them?  I went to their website, but would like something a little more objective.<P>   ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||01-13-2000||05:00 PM||||I'm only slightly familiar with my 3800 but a NA is 205hp and SC is 240. Mine has had the blower "upgraded"(smaller pulley and modified wastegate-don't know the boost)but using the numbers game again I should be 277 at the crank (222 measured at wheels). I was told by a Buick mechanic that 3800 is a very strong motor and could handle a whole bunch of boost. I really don't care why --but it goes like a bat out of **** and gets 30 mpg and should last for a long time.<BR>edit- No turbo lag either  I LIKE IT !!!!<P><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 01-13-2000).]||||reg||1
