Z||000015||terryk||01-14-2000||12:38 AM||||How much boost for 201HP at the wheels? On 9:1 compression? There is not a hope in H**l that I would put more than 6 PSI of boost on 9:1 for $3500. That would take more than 11 PSI to gain that much more HP unless they just kept reving it past the stock HP RPM spec.<P>On 8:1, sure, but the torque will be down. Still a terrible HP/$ ratio. <P>But, hey if you got the crazy money!<P>I'm over spinning an Eaton at 13 PSI on 8:1 in my Ciera and I'm not getting a 100HP increase over NA. <P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000016||Shaun41178||01-14-2000||03:33 PM||||I don't know where this guy got his supercharger kit from but it clearly states on RS Motorsports website that their kit costs $2800 US.  <P>9 Psi is not a lot of boost and the stock engine can take it.  It will wear faster then with no boost but that is the price you pay for high hp.  The designone turbo system runs 9 psi of boost and there are many Fiero owners with this turbo system that still have the factory engine.<P>Terryk:  The engine gained more then 50 hp.  It gained 80 hp at the wheels which is like 230-240 at the crank.  More then enough power to get the Fiero running faster then vettes.<P>As far as hp per dollar sure it is expensive.  But I don't see anyone complaing about the performance dollar per hp of a chip.  People on this list have spent $100 on a chip and get no gain at all.  But they seem happy.  WHy?  People buy a pulley and that has a terrible hp per dollar ratio as well but I for one can attest for improved qtr mile times with it.  There are too many people out there that think there "seat of the pants-ometer" is just as accurate as a clock and all are wrong.  "Yes a chip gave a noticeable improvement in my Fiero"  WRONG.  I have witnessed dyno tests of both the hypertech and ADS chip and both chips gave and astonishing peak of +2 hp.  Also that was not through the whole rpm range.  That was just peak.  At some rpm it actaully made less hp.   <P>The best hp per dollar ratio mod that one can buy for there car is nitrous oxide.  That is the cheapest mod and has the biggest gains.  But not everyone wants nitrous on their engine and would prefer a turbo or super.  And with that comes the cost of one.  I will tell you, you don't pay for the super or turbo kit, you pay for the engineering and design of it.  A lot of research goes into making these kits.  As far as a nitrous kit goes it isn't too hard to make one.  After buying one and see what parts come with it, I could build my own for half the price.  Many of the parts are available locally.  That is why they are much cheaper then the super or turbos.  Good day people||||reg||2
Z||000017||terryk||01-15-2000||12:54 AM||||The price is $3900.00CN or $2785.00US. That's where the two prices come in.<P>I looked at the site. Looks like a Paxton. Does anyone think it's a Vortec? The output is the same as the Paxton's I've seen.<P>They say 80HP increase and 200HP at the wheels. I noticed the dyno for before and after are done differently though. I'd like to know the RPM for that HP. Could have been Honda-ized. There is no RPM/HP graph so I hope they aren't doing the "wind it to 7000 RPM and read the HP quickly!" routine.<P>Looks like they use the 2 bar map and code. No knock sensor.<P>$2800 isn't that bad except for loosing the A/C. The installation pics looked good. Even a Buick SC swap done yourself would cost that. Probably no EO for it.<P>Why couldn't a Vortec mounted backwards in place of the battery and driven from the crank work? It would take some clever brackets. Some of the Vortecs are counter rotating. Raw Vortec is around $1700 I think.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Phil||01-16-2000||04:18 PM||||Horse Power TV ran a segment today on putting a supercharger on a 4.3 Vortec S-10. I wasn't able to catch the starting HP but they ended up with 202HP (no torque #) they said that they gained about 60 HP <P>For what it's worth <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||87 Steve GT||01-16-2000||05:07 PM||||Hmmmmmm.....  ||||reg||6
Z||000020||terryk||01-17-2000||01:31 AM||||Isn't a 4.3L Vortec about 200 HP stock?<P>Also, Buick gets 35HP with about 11 PSI (8 PSI typical) on their 3800's. 9 PSI of boost takes the 2.8L up 80HP. See the math problem?<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||mwbackus||01-17-2000||02:11 AM||||I agree with Terry.  The dyno data displayed for the RSM SC are inconclusive, since they do not appear to be an exact comparison "before and after" using the same chart format.<P>The 4.3L V6 available in the S-10 pickups ranged from a low of 160hp (1990), to around 190hp in more recent model years.  With 14lbs of boost, the Syclone/Typhon turbocharged 4.3 was rated at 280hp, but this was obtained using an 8.35:1 compression ratio.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000022||mrfiero||01-17-2000||02:19 AM||||I believe the stock supercharger on the 3800 is only putting out 5-6 psi of boost.  With the "upgraded" pulley it is closer to 7.5 psi.  It is no where near the 11 psi range....it would likely require an intercooler and it would certainly put out more than 35 extra horses.<P>The newer Vortec 4.3 V-6's put out approx. 195 HP, so a supercharger would have to increase the output to more than 201....unless the engine loses that much power from driveline losses?  These motors are known more for their torque anyways....I'd like to see what kind torque figures it puts out with a supercharger.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Phil||01-17-2000||08:42 AM||||The TV program took readings at the wheels - sales brochures take them at the crank. Way back when there was a "snake oil" product that used "actual dyno charts"to show HP increases for their product.  The before chart was at the wheels and the after was at the crank. They neglected to mention that little bit of info. <BR>Just reread this post and then looked at the dyno charts from RSM (real smooth curve on the after). If they did the wheels before and crank after the resulting HP figures fit very nicely with predicted  results. <p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Phil||01-18-2000||06:36 AM||||Error||||reg||1
Z||000025||terryk||01-18-2000||01:20 PM||||The 92-95 Buicks assume 8 PSI average with 11 PSI max (pulley limited). See the blower section of the manual. I get 13 PSI of boost on my 95 blower using the 92 pulley. That aligns with the Eaton measurements. I did have to drop the advance down 2 degrees at max load to keep the ESC from going too high. With an intercooler, I could probably throw 6 degrees back in. The Buick SC's are far lower compression and the stock Fiero.<P>I'm not saying RSM is lying. I'm saying that I couldn't draw the conclusions they indicate. They could very well could be getting 80HP more. Obviously, GM has a big durability stake. <P>Hey, the $2700US is right in line with other kits. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
